{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>This day I begin.</b> Here is a hint that the sun stood still for Moses in the war against Og, just as it stood still for Joshua. And so they expounded in the tractate Ta'anit: three were granted that the sun should stand still for them — Moses, Joshua, and Nakdimon ben Gurion. Nakdimon — from the Gemara; Joshua, as it is written (Joshua 10:13) ״שמש בגבעון דום״, and it is written (ibid.) ״ויעמוד השמש בחצי השמים״; Moses — from where do we know it? R. Elazar says: it is derived from אחל — אחל: it is written here ״אחל תת פחדך״ and it is written in Joshua (Joshua 10:3) ״אחל גדלך״. R. Yohanan says: it is derived from תת — תת: it is written here ״תת פחדך״ and it is written there (Joshua 10:10) ״ביום תת ה' את האמורי״. R. Shmuel bar Nahmani says: it is derived from the verse itself, as it is written ״אחל תת פחדך ויראתך על פני העמים תחת כל השמים אשר ישמעון שמעך ורגזו וחלו מפניך״ — when did all the nations beneath all the heavens tremble and quake before you? At the time when the sun stood still for Moses — that is to say, the matter became known beneath the entire vault of heaven; and therefore it says ״the ha-yom hazeh״ (this day). And how is it possible that his fear and dread should be given upon the face of the nations beneath all the heavens, except through the standing still of the sun?" "<b>And behold,</b> this is a great wonder and an astounding marvel in the renewal of the creation of the world, because the existence of the world depends upon the movement and rotation of the sphere; and if it were to stand still even for a moment, the world would be destroyed. Yet here the sphere stood still from rotating and the world continued to exist. But such was not the case with Hezekiah, for there the sun did not stand still but turned backward toward the dawn; and the sphere did not cease from rotating, neither forward nor backward, and the miracle was not so great. Therefore it says three, and does not count Hezekiah among them. And likewise the word נקדה indicates this, for it is from the word נקודה (point), as if to say: for three the sun stood still for them at its point — that the sphere stood still from rotating in its usual way." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: that which Joshua said to the sun ״דום״ (be still) and did not say ״עמוד״ (stand), is because the sun, in its course, praises the Holy One, blessed be He; and if it is silenced it cannot proceed, as it is said (Psalms 113:3) ״ממזרח שמש עד מבואו מהלל שם ה'״ — from the hour it rises until the hour it sets, it praises the name of HaShem; and therefore he commanded it to be silent, so that it would stand and remain fixed at its point. And they further expounded: the sun said to Joshua (Joshua 10:13), 'A lesser one does not say to a greater one, דום!' — for I was created on the fourth day and you on the sixth. Joshua said to it: 'A small free man does not say to a great servant, שתוק (be quiet)!' Immediately, ״וידום השמש״ — and the sun was silent; and once it was silent, it stood, as it is said (Joshua 10) ״ויעמד השמש בחצי השמים״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:03.244939155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}